@manninglucas, [the import](https://github.com/google/gvisor/pull/16542/files) includes the capability fix from the approved head, but keeps `tags = ["local"]` on `//runsc/cgroup:cgroup_test`. The [source change](https://github.com/google/gvisor/pull/16496/files) removes that line. With it present Bazel cannot even generate the test’s Go main on a remote worker ([AMD64](https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/34735724-4f08-4272-8288-1c81321339c4); [both architectures](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/actions/runs/41231932306/artifacts/13055965081)).

Could you remove the local tag in the internal change before it lands? The import already points to the head you approved.

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.

@manninglucas, I added a permanent test for a mount resolving after the waiter registers. It verifies that lookup returns the mounted root and leaves the channel open for the queued notification.

Could you review the new head, [b2cbea854](https://github.com/google/gvisor/commit/b2cbea854), following your [earlier review](https://github.com/google/gvisor/pull/16594#pullrequestreview-5758483291)?

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.

@parth-opensrc, `b.totalMem` can be larger than the cgroup limit Linux enforces. On the earlier revision I set a parent’s `memory.max` to 512 MiB and its child’s to 2 GiB. The [cgroup reader](https://github.com/google/gvisor/blob/5068ecb10/runsc/cgroup/cgroup_v2.go#L413) returned 2 GiB, and `Suspend` set Go’s soft memory limit to 1 GiB. It did the same with two unlimited levels below the 512 MiB parent. A 256 MiB child correctly got a 128 MiB Go limit.

Could we land [#16767](https://github.com/google/gvisor/pull/16767) before relying on this value to suspend GC? It fixes the shared cgroup reader. Taking the smallest visible limit gave the first two cases a 256 MiB Go limit and left the control unchanged. [Results from a real cgroup-v2 hierarchy](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/actions/runs/41166214724/artifacts/13029347885). These measure the limit passed to Go; no workload was driven to OOM.

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.

@SecretSun, the same client port across successive checkpoints points to one recurring connection tuple. Our July test kept the accepted connection open at each checkpoint; it didn’t cover a server-side connection in TIME_WAIT. The recurring tuple is a lead, but it doesn’t establish which entry was nil.

For the GKE team: if an affected checkpoint is still available, [the July writer](https://github.com/google/gvisor/blob/9f653e577/runsc/boot/restore.go#L696-L704) records its version. `runsc statefile -get runsc_version <statefile>` [reads just that field](https://github.com/google/gvisor/blob/9f653e577/runsc/cmd/statefile.go#L130-L143) without restoring the pod. Could you map that label and the build that restored the pod to your internal revisions, then check for [the checkpoint timer fix](https://github.com/google/gvisor/commit/2337c1f9f) and [the restored RNG fix](https://github.com/google/gvisor/commit/a6d2142e3)? The RNG fix matches the separate RNG stack trace; it doesn’t establish the cause of the nil endpoint. No checkpoint contents or new reproduction are needed here.

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.

@cknolla @learnrahulrai-ui, would you consider fixing this inside `DeepCopyForForwarding`? [This two-line change](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/commit/8959e5c44) reads the network and transport header lengths from the packet metadata. Calling `Slice` just to get those lengths is what moves the source storage: at that point `BufferSince` still holds a reference to the same chunk.

With that change, the IPv4 caller can keep its original header, and the forwarded packet still has independently writable headers. [The same test on both revisions](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/actions/runs/41199044377/artifacts/13041728253) checks both properties: stock moves the source header, while the change passes. It would be useful to run your concurrent TTL test against this version too.

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.

@kekawia, I reproduced the 30-second read on master with your flags. The client had read 2,576,304 of 2,800,000 bytes. The link dropped all 16 transmissions of the next 1,200 bytes. On the second RTO the sender saw `xmitCount=16`, above its retry limit of 15, and closed with `ErrTimeout` before sending that segment again. Fast retransmissions count toward that limit. The client still advertised about 4 MiB and waited until its own read deadline. [Byte and timer trace](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/actions/runs/42242371414/artifacts/13516598012) · [Retry check](https://github.com/google/gvisor/blob/f51a205a8/pkg/tcpip/transport/tcp/snd.go#L695-L703)

On your PR the same seed also had two RTOs and finished in 985 ms. All 12 heavy transfers in my earlier run finished on your PR, so I can confirm the cause on master only.

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.
